{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>A portion like a portion they shall eat.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): it says that the priests shall eat a portion like a portion — everything given to them — and they shall divide equally, portion for portion, in the skins and in the flesh and in the goats of the sin-offering and in all the gifts." "<b>Beside his sales upon the fathers.</b> Aside from what one of them may have sold — a house he inherited from his fathers, in the manner of what is written (Leviticus 25): ״ויצא ממכר בית ועיר אחזתו ביובל״ — they may not say to him: 'Behold, you have become rich by selling your house; you shall not take a portion like a portion with us.' This is the meaning of 'beside his sales upon the fathers'; thus explained the sage Rabbi Abraham, of blessed memory.\n\nAnd the reason the pericope began 'And when the Levite comes' — its meaning is the priest, who is of the tribe of Levi; it is not speaking of a Levite literally, for it says 'and he shall serve in the name of HaShem his Elohim,' and it is the priest who serves, not the Levite.\n\nAnd what it says, 'and he shall come with all the desire of his soul,' teaches that a priest is permitted to offer his offerings even in a mishmar (watch) that is not his own; and likewise on the festivals they offer the offerings that come on account of the festival even in a mishmar that is not theirs." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> 'A portion like a portion they shall eat,' teaches that they divide in the skins and in the flesh of the goats of sin-offerings. One might think even in matters that do not come on account of the festival, such as the daily offerings and the additional offerings of Shabbat and vows and freewill-offerings — therefore it says 'beside his sales upon the fathers': apart from what the fathers sold in the days of David and Solomon, when the mishmarot (watches) were fixed and they sold to one another: 'You take your watch and I shall take my watch' — thus far." "<b>And if</b> you gain insight into the division of the mishmarot, then you will grasp a wondrous matter. For since all the priests as one share equally in everything given to them and eat 'a portion like a portion,' what did the fathers and the great ones of the world in the days of David and Solomon see to act thus, and what had come to them that they agreed to divide them into mishmarot and to have them rotate every single week, saying 'You take your watch and I my watch'? And further, for what reason did they call each and every group serving week by week by the name 'mishmar'? And further, for what reason did they divide them into 24 fixed mishmarot, and the mishmarot could not be transferred to any other watch in any manner or circumstance whatsoever — as is said at the end of tractate Sukkah regarding Miriam bat Bilgah, that they sealed her window, but they did not transfer her?\n\nBut know and understand that the priests, the ministers of the Temple — their service below is a likeness of the service above. Just as above there are mishmarot of the ministering angels who serve and praise the Holy One, blessed be He, so the fathers divided the priests into mishmarot to perform their service. And they established 24 mishmarot corresponding to three groups above, each of which is 24, as hinted in Sefer ha-Bahir. And the mishmarot were fixed, for so are those above fixed all the days that the Will abides in them. And they would rotate and return in cycle, for so above — the Holy One, blessed be He, creates a group of angels each day, and they recite song and depart. And each watch, as it departs, would bless: 'May He whose name dwells in this House cause to dwell among you love, peace, and friendship' — for so above there is among them no jealousy or hatred, but love, peace, and friendship.\n\nBehold, the matter of the division of the mishmarot was a counsel devised by the fathers in the days of David and Solomon, done with a known intention and wondrous wisdom."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:49:20.001538478Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}